\title{hexmap: Convert a Map into a Hexagon Tile Map}
\description{
\if{html}{\figure{logo.png}{options: style='float: right' alt='logo' width='120'}}

Converts a map of regions, as an 'sf' object, into a map of equally sized hexagons with one hexagon for each region. Each region is allocated the hexagon that minimises the total squared displacement of all regions, so regions keep their relative positions, while distant islands are tiled separately. Groups of regions (such as states) can be kept together using one of several layouts, including a diffusion cartogram (Gastner and Newman, 2004) \doi{10.1073/pnas.0400280101} and spatially ordered treemaps (Wood and Dykes, 2008) \doi{10.1109/TVCG.2008.165}. This makes small but important regions as visible as large, sparsely populated ones.
}
